            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a9-8cc0-df30502d11fb",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Logging",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " is the process of recording information about your application’s behavior — like what it's doing, errors it encounters, or important events. In Spring Boot, logging helps developers "
        ],
        [
          "understand what’s happening inside the application",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " at runtime. Spring Boot uses "
        ],
        [
          "SLF4J",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " (a logging façade) and "
        ],
        [
          "Logback",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " (a logging implementation) by default to make logging flexible and powerful."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8013-a4ca-cf6aca741e34",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a0-b0db-d33948f01e60",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "2. "
        ],
        [
          "Key Interview Points",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80d6-9bfa-cfa47ced3293",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "✅ "
        ],
        [
          "What is SLF4J and Logback?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80d6-893d-c8ca1de3e8c7",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "SLF4J is like a “universal plug” for logging — it lets you use any logging backend (like Logback or Log4j) without changing your code. Logback is the default engine that actually writes the logs in Spring Boot."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8052-9b80-d2524e07f174",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "✅ "
        ],
        [
          "How does Spring Boot configure logging automatically?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-806e-befa-cb39d472f006",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "Spring Boot auto-configures Logback and sets up basic logging out of the box — no setup required unless you want to customize it."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-804b-bfb3-e99ea9baae73",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "✅ "
        ],
        [
          "How to write logs in your code?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-809c-8ce7-cee01ff6df9b",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "You use a logger instance (like "
            ],
            [
              "logger.info(\"User logged in\")",
              [
                [
                  "c"
                ]
              ]
            ],
            [
              ") to log messages at different levels: "
            ],
            [
              "INFO",
              [
                [
                  "c"
                ]
              ]
            ],
            [
              ", "
            ],
            [
              "DEBUG",
              [
                [
                  "c"
                ]
              ]
            ],
            [
              ", "
            ],
            [
              "ERROR",
              [
                [
                  "c"
                ]
              ]
            ],
            [
              ", etc."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80a7-9e5a-dd90476336d8",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "✅ "
        ],
        [
          "Where do logs go?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ],
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80b4-a688-e3d84fe92cfc",
          "type": "text",
          "richText": [
            [
              "By default: logs go to the console. You can configure them to also go into files, and format them using "
            ],
            [
              "application.properties",
              [
                [
                  "c"
                ]
              ]
            ],
            [
              "."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
