    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8031-86b4-fc893e984f6e",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: "
        ],
        [
          "What is SLF4J and how does Spring Boot use it for logging?",
          [
            [
              "i"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c8-9c19-fe970c627215",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " SLF4J is a logging façade — it lets you write logging code that works with multiple logging frameworks underneath. Spring Boot uses SLF4J with Logback as the default logging backend. It auto-configures basic logging so you can just use a logger in your code without extra setup."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80f8-8abd-d4331bedccd0",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80fe-8c5f-fd0248f3641a",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: "
        ],
        [
          "How can you configure different logging levels in Spring Boot?",
          [
            [
              "i"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80e9-8d92-f284e6f9d9e7",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " You can set logging levels in the "
        ],
        [
          "application.properties",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " file using "
        ],
        [
          "logging.level.package-name=LEVEL",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ". For example, "
        ],
        [
          "logging.level.com.example=DEBUG",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " sets DEBUG level for that package. You can also direct logs to a file and format the output."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c7-a303-e4557d2d1f40",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80c9-a089-e05190a652bc",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: "
        ],
        [
          "What's the difference between SLF4J and Logback?",
          [
            [
              "i"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-8097-b285-ec38dd8c1c80",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " SLF4J is the API you code against, and Logback is the actual implementation that processes and writes the logs. Think of SLF4J as the interface, and Logback as the engine."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20924eb1-ed54-80cd-83d1-fcc671db1301",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations",
      "type": "header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Useful logs and configuration"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations-b0",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Use the SLF4J placeholder form, such as log.info(\"Saved journal {}\", id), so the log message and its arguments stay separate."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations-b1",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Set levels for the package that owns the problem. Global DEBUG can produce too much output and expose sensitive values."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations-b2",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A logging configuration controls appenders, levels and formats. With Boot’s default Logback support, logback-spring.xml allows Boot-specific configuration such as profiles."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations-b3",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Use a correlation ID to connect related request and background-work messages. A nearby timestamp alone does not prove that messages describe the same operation."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations-b4",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Log the operation and a safe identifier. Do not log passwords, tokens, complete personal journal text or secret-bearing URLs."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-logging-operations-b5",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Log a caught exception with its throwable when its stack trace is needed. A log message alone does not propagate the failure to callers."
        ]
      ]
    }
